##

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.600 line:91%
There's an asterisk
in the title of this video.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.000 line:85%
Of course there's an asterisk.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:05.600 line:91%
There has to be.
And I'll be honest...

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.240 line:91%
I had a whole script
ready to go here

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.520 line:91%
as the sun sets over the
Atacama Desert in Chile.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.720 line:91%
But, I've found so much
more than I expected

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.640 line:91%
over the last day and a half.
So many stories.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.800 line:91%
I've been exhausted
both from the altitude

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.240 line:91%
– we're three kilometres
up here –

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.080 line:91%
and from dashing around
with handheld cameras

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.360 line:85%
trying to capture everything.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.120 line:91%
So, what's going to
happen now is

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:29.680 line:91%
we're going to cut to
a version of me

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:32.000 line:91%
in a couple weeks' time
who is coherent

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.760 line:91%
and who's had time
to process all this.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.760 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
[Caption+ by JS*
caption.plus | @caption_plus]

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.000 line:85%
<i>Here's the story.</i>

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.480 line:91%
I got an invitation to visit
the Extremely Large Telescope.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.720 line:91%
Scientists love giving
telescopes names like that.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.720 line:91%
It's a telescope.
It's extremely large.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:48.760 line:85%
The invite was from

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.640 line:91%
the UK Science and
Technology Facilities Council

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.320 line:91%
and the European
Southern Observatory.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:53.520 line:85%
They arranged everything,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:55.440 line:91%
<i>but they have
no editorial control</i>

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.640 line:85%
<i>over what I'm saying.</i>

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.160 line:85%
<i>And I paid for my own travel.</i>

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.240 line:85%
Those flights were expensive.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.280 align:start size:94% position:6% line:91%
So there'll be an advert
for NordVPN later on,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.160 align:start size:94% position:6% line:91%
because it was actually useful
to me while I was in Chile.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.800 line:91%
Anyway, the
Atacama Desert is

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.640 line:91%
<i>the most desolate place
I've ever been to.</i>

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:12.440 line:91%
<i>Rolling hills
of stone and rock</i>

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:14.400 line:91%
<i>for hour after hour
of driving.</i>

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.800 line:91%
<i>I'm sure there's life
out there somewhere,</i>

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.680 line:85%
<i>but we didn't see any of it.</i>

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.680 line:91%
Eventually, we arrive at
the base camp at Paranal.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.520 line:91%
<i>Let me give you the
lay of the land.</i>

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:24.680 line:91%
<i>At the bottom of the hill
is the Residence,</i>

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:27.920 line:91%
<i>where visiting scientists
and non-scientists stay.</i>

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.120 line:91%
<i>Not a hotel.
Very definitely not a hotel.</i>

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:32.320 line:91%
<i>You can't pay for a
room there if you try.</i>

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.120 line:85%
<i>It's more like university dorms,</i>

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.760 line:91%
<i>if university dorms ever
won architectural awards.</i>

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.080 line:91%
<i>That's at the
bottom of the hill,</i>

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.320 line:91%
<i>along with all the support
buildings and structures</i>

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.760 line:91%
<i>that are needed to
support life out there.</i>

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.960 line:91%
<i>Up at the top of the hill
is the Very Large Telescope,</i>

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:47.640 line:85%
<i>built about 20 years ago.</i>

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.040 line:91%
<i>And a few kilometres away,
a little dot</i>

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.920 line:91%
<i>on a flattened-off hill
on the horizon:</i>

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.600 line:91%
<i>that's the Extremely Large
Telescope, the new one.</i>

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.520 line:85%
<i>And that's likely going to be</i>

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.680 line:91%
the largest optical telescope
that will ever be built.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.760 line:85%
<i>But to understand why that is,</i>

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.200 line:85%
<i>I need to show you around.</i>

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.400 line:85%
<i>Our first stop is at</i>

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.480 line:91%
the Very Large Telescope
– the VLT –

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.840 line:91%
up on the hill about 2km
from base camp.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.200 line:85%
<i>In theory, you can walk it.</i>

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.920 line:91%
<i>There's a path called
the Star Track, but...</i>

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.880 line:91%
<i>I didn't feel like
getting altitude sickness.</i>

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:18.880 line:91%
<i>The VLT has been operational
for more than 20 years now.</i>

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.200 line:91%
<i>Results from it have
won Nobel Prizes.</i>

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.560 line:91%
<i>It was the first telescope
to take an actual picture</i>

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.480 line:91%
<i>of a planet around
another star.</i>

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.560 line:91%
It tested Einstein's
general relativity

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:28.840 line:85%
<i>by tracking a star around</i>

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:31.240 line:91%
<i>the supermassive black hole
at the centre of the galaxy.</i>

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.680 line:91%
<i>And it's actually
several telescopes.</i>

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.280 line:91%
<i>The four big ones are called
the Unit Telescopes, or UTs.</i>

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.880 line:85%
<i>And we were visiting UT4.</i>

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.520 line:91%
Are we good to
just go in? Okay.

00:02:42.520 --> 00:02:44.240 line:85%
Oh, wow!

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.160 line:85%
<i>(laughs)</i>

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.640 line:91%
<c.yellow>So, at Paranal, we
have four UT telescopes.</c>

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.760 line:91%
<c.yellow>These are the large
telescopes we have here.</c>

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:53.800 line:85%
<c.yellow>Where the primary mirror,</c>

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.920 line:91%
<c.yellow>this thing that you see here,
underneath us here,</c>

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.120 line:85%
<c.yellow>is 8.2 metres in diameter.</c>

00:02:58.120 --> 00:02:59.640 line:85%
And that is a mirror...

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.040 line:85%
very, very scientifically precise,

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.320 line:91%
but the same kind
of optical mirror

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.600 line:91%
that you would have just to
do your makeup in the morning

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.840 line:85%
or something like that.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.440 line:91%
<c.yellow>Um, yes, yes.</c>
<i>(laughs)</i>

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.080 line:91%
Lesson one:
Do not compare

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:11.280 line:91%
the extremely expensive
scientific instrument

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.680 line:85%
to a makeup mirror.

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.120 line:85%
The important thing is that

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:17.240 line:91%
the telescopes here
are optical telescopes.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.320 line:91%
That's the first reason
for the asterisk.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.240 line:91%
There are already
bigger radio telescopes.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.800 line:91%
<i>I was lucky enough to visit
Arecibo before the collapse,</i>

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:27.320 line:91%
<i>and I recently visited
Parkes in Australia.</i>

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.640 line:85%
<i>They're all much bigger.</i>

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:31.920 line:91%
But they collect
radio waves, not light.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:33.680 line:91%
They pick up very
different frequencies,

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.840 line:91%
and they're useful for
observing different objects

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.480 line:85%
and doing different science.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.320 line:91%
There's stuff
you can do with radio

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:42.120 line:91%
that you can't do with
optical, and vice versa.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.920 line:91%
Radio telescopes have
to be much larger

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.840 line:91%
because the wavelengths
of the microwaves

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:48.320 line:91%
and radio waves
they pick up

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.680 line:91%
range from millimetres
to tens of metres.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.680 line:91%
But they don't have
to be as precise.

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:55.320 line:91%
As long as you build
a big bowl

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.040 line:91%
<i>that's the right shape,
you don't need to make</i>

00:03:57.040 --> 00:03:58.880 line:85%
<i>a perfect optical mirror finish.</i>

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.200 line:91%
It will still work.
Arecibo's surface was just

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.960 line:91%
<i>kind of rough mesh,
but that still worked.</i>

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.240 line:91%
Optical astronomy looks for
visible light or infrared.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:08.680 line:91%
The stuff we can see
with the naked eye,

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.960 line:85%
or close to that.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:11.280 line:91%
The wavelengths
are from about

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.160 line:91%
100 micrometres to
100 nanometres.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.160 line:91%
<i>And so the optics have
to be so, so precise,</i>

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.080 line:85%
<i>just like any other camera lens.</i>

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:20.600 line:85%
<i>Well actually, more precise,</i>

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.320 line:91%
<i>because mirrors this big
have other problems.</i>

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.840 line:91%
<c.yellow>When you get to
this large telescope,</c>

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>it's actually extremely difficult</c>

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.160 line:91%
<c.yellow>to keep the mirror stable
and in the same position.</c>

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.640 line:91%
<c.yellow>It has 150 actuators
underneath it.</c>

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:34.880 line:91%
It's like poking the
mirror ever so slightly.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.120 line:85%
That must be microns.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:37.880 line:91%
That must be a tiny,
tiny amount it's moving.

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:39.080 line:85%
<c.yellow>Yeah, and this is basically</c>

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:41.480 line:91%
<c.yellow>to take into account
gravity of the mirror</c>

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.560 line:91%
<c.yellow>and the temperature difference
we have within here,</c>

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:45.200 line:91%
<c.yellow>because when
you move the mirror,</c>

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:46.960 line:91%
<c.yellow>then from gravity, then
the shape of the mirror</c>

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:48.560 line:85%
<c.yellow>is also changing as well.</c>

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.360 line:91%
<i>That's called
"active optics",</i>

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.040 line:91%
<i>deforming the mirror
ever so slightly</i>

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.560 line:91%
<i>to deal with the effects
of gravity as you tilt it,</i>

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.280 line:91%
<i>and from thermal expansion
as the temperature changes.</i>

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.080 line:91%
That's how accurate
this has to be.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:02.760 line:91%
We'll come back to that later,
'cause I got to see the mirror

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.120 line:91%
and those actuators
close up, but first...

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.520 line:91%
We've got to explain what
that mirror actually does.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:08.720 line:91%
They were getting it
ready for the night,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:10.080 line:85%
so they had to test it.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.880 line:85%
Because the whole thing moves.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:14.160 line:91%
Oh, oh!
<c.yellow>We're moving.</c>

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.160 line:91%
That's way faster than
I thought it was gonna be.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.760 line:91%
<c.yellow>We basically
test the rotation.</c>

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.960 line:91%
<c.yellow>We test all of the
movements of the telescopes.</c>

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:22.360 line:91%
<c.yellow>We check for the
safety of the system,</c>

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.480 line:91%
<c.yellow>before we're going to start
the nighttime operations.</c>

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.160 line:91%
<c.yellow>You can't hear the
movement, right?</c>

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.760 line:85%
This is so fast!

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.080 line:91%
Oh, we're going
back the other way.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.040 line:85%
I'm just giggling.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.280 line:91%
<c.yellow>This is several hundred
tons of material.</c>

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:33.880 line:91%
<c.yellow>It's moving like this,
and basically</c>

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:35.320 line:91%
<c.yellow>it's kind of floating
on top of</c>

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.560 line:91%
<c.yellow>this very small amount
of oil film</c>

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:39.640 line:91%
<c.yellow>that's underneath here.
Because during the night,</c>

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:41.000 line:91%
<c.yellow>when we're doing the
scientific observations,</c>

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.520 line:85%
<c.yellow>we may be staying on one target.</c>

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.160 line:91%
<c.yellow>So observing one galaxy
for several hours.</c>

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>Because the Earth is rotating,</c>

00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:48.840 line:91%
<c.yellow>so therefore we need
to move the telescope</c>

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:51.520 line:91%
<c.yellow>so that we keep pointing at
exactly the same position.</c>

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:54.200 line:91%
<c.yellow>So this movement needs
to be extremely smooth,</c>

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:55.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>because we don't want to add</c>

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:57.680 line:91%
<c.yellow>any additional movement
onto the observations</c>

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.440 line:91%
<c.yellow>when we're observing
our science targets.</c>

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.440 line:91%
<i>Rotation test complete,
now the tilt.</i>

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.160 line:91%
<c.yellow>We're going to
open the dome...</c>

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.120 line:91%
<c.yellow>and we want to put the
mirror in such a way</c>

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.280 line:91%
<c.yellow>that if anything falls
down from the dome,</c>

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.640 line:85%
<c.yellow>that the probability of that</c>

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:11.960 line:91%
<c.yellow>actually falling on the
mirror is extremely low.</c>

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.120 line:91%
<c.yellow>In the middle of the desert,
constantly you're having dust</c>

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.400 line:91%
<c.yellow>that's accumulating
onto the mirror.</c>

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:18.520 line:91%
<c.yellow>I mean, large things
falling onto it</c>

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.080 line:91%
<c.yellow>is extremely rare.</c>
<i>(chuckles)</i>

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.520 line:91%
I just looked over there.
Oh, wow!

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.440 line:91%
So the light would
come in from a star.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:27.080 line:91%
<c.yellow>Yes.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Hit the main mirror,</c>

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:30.160 line:91%
which is the big
reflective thing there...

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:31.880 line:85%
back to M2 just here.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:32.720 line:85%
<c.yellow>Exactly.</c>

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:34.960 line:91%
And then down
to M3 in the middle.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:37.720 line:91%
<c.yellow>It can rotate to send it
to any two at the instrument,</c>

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.760 position:66% line:91%
<c.yellow>or it opens to send it to
the instrument that's below the M1 mirror.</c>

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:41.760 line:85%
Right.

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:43.880 line:91%
There's a lot of very bad
animations out there,

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.440 line:91%
which show
satellite transmissions

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:46.520 line:85%
and stuff like that

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:48.800 line:91%
beaming not into the
bowl of the dish,

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.120 line:91%
<i>but to the detector,
the thing that sticks out.</i>

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.800 line:91%
<i>And that is the wrong way round.
That's really important.</i>

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.520 line:91%
Light bounces off
M1, the bowl,

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:59.040 line:91%
<i>and gets focused
onto the focal point.</i>

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:01.080 line:91%
<i>And then here it gets
bounced down again,</i>

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.720 line:91%
<i>either to the
equipment at the sides</i>

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.320 line:85%
or down into the basement.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.640 line:91%
Light's going to
come in and hit M1.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:08.440 line:91%
<c.yellow>Yes.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>But isn't M2</c>

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:10.040 line:91%
in the way of what
you're trying to observe?

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.720 line:91%
<c.yellow>No, it doesn't, because
you have parallel light beams</c>

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.160 line:85%
<c.yellow>that are arriving to the M1.</c>

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:14.400 line:91%
It's not going to come
across on camera,

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:15.280 line:85%
I don't think...

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:16.960 line:91%
But there is quite a little
white mark just there,

00:07:16.960 --> 00:07:19.520 line:91%
and a lot of dust
on the mirror.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.520 line:91%
How much is that going to
affect the observations?

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:23.640 line:91%
<c.yellow>For any given object
of a given brightness,</c>

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.400 line:85%
<c.yellow>as the mirror gets dirtier,</c>

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:27.200 line:91%
<c.yellow>then you'll have to
observe for longer</c>

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.600 line:91%
<c.yellow>to get the same amount of
light on your detector.</c>

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:32.480 line:91%
<c.yellow>We recoat the mirror once
every two or three years,</c>

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:34.120 line:85%
<c.yellow>maintain the reflectivity.</c>

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:35.520 line:85%
<i>They do use dry ice</i>

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:36.960 line:91%
to dust the mirrors
