00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.240 line:91%
Yeah.
<c.green>On the guide star.</c>

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:57.400 line:91%
<i>So, in short,
they shoot the laser up,</i>

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:58.560 line:85%
<i>see how it wobbles,</i>

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.240 line:85%
<i>and then do maths</i>

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:02.480 line:91%
to subtract that wobble
from the observation.

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:04.600 line:91%
Look, I'm gonna say
"they do maths"

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:05.880 line:91%
at a few points
during this video,

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.440 line:91%
because it's the
sort of calculation

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:10.080 line:91%
that people spend
years learning about.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.280 line:91%
Suffice it to say,
they do maths,

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:14.360 line:91%
work out how the laser
guide stars are moving,

00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:17.840 line:91%
and then physically move
and distort the M2 mirror

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:20.480 line:91%
to subtract that
atmospheric distortion

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.640 line:91%
from the actual stuff
they're looking at.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:24.440 line:85%
So, now you know

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:26.000 line:91%
<i>how a modern
optical telescope works,</i>

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:27.840 line:91%
<i>and how precise and
difficult the work is.</i>

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:29.520 line:91%
<i>That's the first thing
you need to know</i>

00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:30.480 line:85%
to understand why

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.480 line:91%
the Extremely Large
Telescope, the new one,

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.600 line:91%
is probably the biggest
that will ever be built.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:39.640
<i>The next day,
we're able to go</i>

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:40.680 line:85%
down into the basement

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:42.320 line:85%
underneath the four telescopes.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.120 line:91%
Because when I said
that the VLT was

00:15:44.120 --> 00:15:46.080 line:85%
four Unit Telescopes...

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.360 line:91%
<i>Yes it is, but they can
all act as one,</i>

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:50.000 line:85%
<i>called the VLTI:</i>

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.320 line:91%
<i>Very Large Telescope
Interferometer.</i>

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:53.920 line:85%
There is a lot of competition

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:55.800 line:91%
for 'biggest telescope
in the world'.

00:15:55.800 --> 00:15:57.840 line:91%
There's things like the
Event Horizon Telescope,

00:15:57.840 --> 00:15:59.920 line:91%
which is a network
of radio telescopes

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:01.480 line:91%
all around the globe.
They all point at

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:03.000 line:91%
the same thing
at the same time,

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:04.560 line:91%
and because
they are so far apart

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.240 line:85%
on other sides of the planet,

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.760 line:91%
the astronomers can record
the data that's received,

00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:11.120 line:91%
analyse it with signal
processing algorithms

00:16:11.120 --> 00:16:13.600 line:91%
and supercomputers,
and do maths,

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:17.720 line:91%
and create a virtual telescope
that is the size of the planet.

00:16:17.720 --> 00:16:19.600 line:85%
<i>That's called interferometry,</i>

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:20.840 line:85%
<i>because the maths is all about</i>

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.000 line:91%
<i>how those signals
interfere with each other.</i>

00:16:23.000 --> 00:16:25.000 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>We basically
completely change</c>

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:26.640 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>the way that
we handle the data</c>

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.120 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>the minute we start
combining these beams.</c>

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:30.200 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>We're thinking about
interfering waves of light</c>

00:16:30.200 --> 00:16:31.640 line:85%
<c.colorFF99FF>rather than an image.</c>

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:34.520 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>And the pattern we get from
the interference of that light</c>

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.960 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>is where we extract all of
the information that we want.</c>

00:16:37.960 --> 00:16:41.440 line:91%
<i>Radio astronomy can do that
by recording the radio waves,</i>

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:43.200 line:91%
and then processing
it all afterwards.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:44.320 line:85%
But remember how I said

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.360 line:91%
the wavelength
of radio is longer,

00:16:46.360 --> 00:16:48.880 line:91%
and the telescopes don't
have to be so precise?

00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:51.080 line:91%
Well, turns out
a lot of other things

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.360 line:91%
don't have to be so
precise for that either.

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:54.440 line:85%
<c.colorFF99FF>Atmospheric variations are</c>

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:56.640 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>a huge limitation
for us in the optical</c>

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.040 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>because, you know, the
atmosphere is full of water.</c>

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:01.960 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>It's full of things
that vary very quickly</c>

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:03.880 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>at optical and
infrared wavelengths.</c>

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:07.640 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>The atmosphere is not
varying so crazily</c>

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.040 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>at radio and
submillimetre wavelengths.</c>

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:11.720 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>So you have—
you're able to maintain</c>

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:12.680 line:85%
<c.colorFF99FF>all that phase information.</c>

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:14.240 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>It doesn't get
completely smeared out</c>

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:16.440 line:85%
<c.colorFF99FF>by the atmospheric variations.</c>

00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:17.520 line:85%
<i>You know if there's a party</i>

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.280 line:91%
with a really loud
sound system a way away?

00:17:20.280 --> 00:17:21.560 line:91%
Then you won't hear
the treble,

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:23.000 line:91%
you won't hear the
tss-tss-tss-tss.

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:23.880 line:85%
But you will hear the

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:25.840 line:91%
thump-thump-thump
of the bass?

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.480 line:85%
Radio waves, and...

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:29.640 line:91%
Apologies to physicists
watching this,

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.440 line:85%
but radio waves are

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.000 line:91%
the bass of the
electromagnetic spectrum.

00:17:34.000 --> 00:17:36.680 line:91%
They travel just fine
through the atmosphere

00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:38.240 line:85%
with minimal distortion.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:39.480 line:91%
That's why nobody
bothers sending

00:17:39.480 --> 00:17:41.400 line:85%
radio telescopes into space.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:42.480 line:85%
We don't need to.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.680 line:91%
<i>But at optical frequencies,
it's different.</i>

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.080 line:91%
<i>All the information that you
need to do interferometry,</i>

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:50.480 line:91%
<i>that information gets
smudged much more easily</i>

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:52.120 line:91%
<i>as it passes through
the atmosphere.</i>

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.240 line:91%
<i>And that's not even
the worst problem.</i>

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:56.160 line:85%
For light, there is not

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:58.640 line:91%
a computer in
the world fast enough

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:00.440 line:91%
to deal with
all that information.

00:18:00.440 --> 00:18:03.320 line:91%
And there is not a clock in
the world precise enough.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:05.040 line:85%
Even radio interferometry

00:18:05.040 --> 00:18:07.200 line:91%
requires very precise
atomic clocks

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:09.120 line:85%
synchronised across the globe.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.600 line:91%
But there just isn't
a clock in the world

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:12.840 line:85%
accurate enough to do that

00:18:12.840 --> 00:18:15.600 line:91%
for the micrometre wavelengths
or nanometre wavelengths,

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:18.560 line:91%
and terahertz
frequencies of light.

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:20.000 line:85%
A radio wave...

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.600 line:85%
You can just record it.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:23.320 line:91%
Even before digital
computers were a thing,

00:18:23.320 --> 00:18:24.920 line:91%
you could just record
a radio wave

00:18:24.920 --> 00:18:27.400 line:91%
onto magnetic tape.
Every single detail of it.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:30.320 line:91%
Every frequency, every
little nuance, everything.

00:18:30.320 --> 00:18:32.880 line:91%
Heck, a boombox from
the '80s can do that.

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.720 line:91%
When people used to tape
songs off the radio,

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.040 line:91%
that is a very rough
and imprecise

00:18:37.040 --> 00:18:39.120 line:85%
version of radio astronomy.

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.440 line:91%
<i>But for light, there is
not a computer in the world</i>

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:43.880 line:91%
<i>fast enough
to record that data.</i>

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.800 line:91%
<i>Not with the accuracy
required for interferometry.</i>

00:18:47.160 --> 00:18:48.560 line:85%
<i>So instead...</i>

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.480 line:91%
<i>they do it physically
in real time.</i>

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:52.360 line:85%
<i>Remember when I said</i>

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:53.880 line:91%
<i>that the light from
the Unit Telescopes</i>

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.920 line:91%
<i>could be sent off to the
equipment at the side,</i>

00:18:55.920 --> 00:18:57.800 line:85%
<i>or down into the basement?</i>

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:59.360 line:85%
<i>Let's go see the basement.</i>

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:01.480 line:85%
So where are we headed now?

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:04.320 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>We are going inside
what we call the coudé room.</c>

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:05.120 line:85%
Coudé?

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:06.800 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>Yeah, this is
the room containing</c>

00:19:06.800 --> 00:19:10.240 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>the adaptive optics system
for the interferometer.</c>

00:19:10.240 --> 00:19:12.240 line:85%
And coudé means...

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.080 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>Elbow.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Elbow, in French.</c>

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:15.520 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>In French.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Okay. <i>(laughs)</i></c>

00:19:15.800 --> 00:19:17.520 line:91%
So I'm guessing
from the name

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.840 line:85%
that this is a movable joint?

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:23.120 line:91%
A hinge that can change
the direction of the light?

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:25.040 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>Yes. Then this,
the coudé,</c>

00:19:25.040 --> 00:19:26.480 line:85%
<c.colorFFCC88>for us, the coudé path,</c>

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:28.840 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>is what we call
the nine mirrors.</c>

00:19:29.080 --> 00:19:30.760 line:91%
That's a serious
warning sign, that is.

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.400 line:91%
<i>(chuckles nervously)</i>
<c.colorFFCC88>Yeah.</c>

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:35.360 line:85%
Oh!

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:37.880 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>Okay, we are
under the telescope,</c>

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.680 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>and we are under what
we call the coudé path.</c>

00:19:40.920 --> 00:19:43.720 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>And this is really where
we receive the light,</c>

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:46.440 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>which will be,
let's call it organised,</c>

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.040 line:91%
<c.colorFFCC88>in order to bring it
towards the VLTI.</c>

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:50.920 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>The light comes into
each of the telescopes,</c>

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:53.040 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>it gets bounced around the
mirrors of the delay tunnel,</c>

00:19:53.040 --> 00:19:55.520 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>gets funnelled into
the VLTI lab,</c>

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:57.200 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>gets funnelled
into the instrument,</c>

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:58.480 line:91%
<c.colorFF99FF>and that's where
it gets combined.</c>

00:19:58.480 --> 00:20:00.800 line:91%
<i>So the light beam
isn't recorded anywhere.</i>

00:20:00.800 --> 00:20:03.560 line:91%
<i>It's just bounced into tiny
tunnels from each telescope.</i>

00:20:03.560 --> 00:20:06.360 line:91%
<i>And its next stop is
a 150 metre long</i>

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:08.080 line:85%
<i>human accessible tunnel</i>

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:10.080 line:91%
to synchronise
all the beams.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:11.680 line:85%
<i>The delay lines.</i>

00:20:11.680 --> 00:20:14.360 line:91%
So these tunnels
run all the way

00:20:14.360 --> 00:20:17.480 line:91%
from all the telescopes
to a central point?

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:18.600 line:91%
Oh?
<c.yellow>Sorry.</c>

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:21.720 line:91%
<c.yellow>Cannot cross to
this side of the room.</c>

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.760 line:91%
Okay, my entire
body needs to stay...

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.760 line:85%
<c.yellow>This is the boundary.</c>

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:27.240 line:91%
Alright, thank you.
<i>(chuckles)</i> Sorry.

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:29.160 line:91%
Okay, so I'm not going to
go near their equipment

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:31.000 line:91%
if I'm told not to.
But in my head, I'm like,

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:32.560 line:85%
that's a little strange.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.960 line:91%
<i>That looks like fairly
heavy infrastructure.</i>

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:36.640 line:91%
<i>Surely I can't do any damage
if I accidentally just...</i>

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:38.680 line:85%
bump up against it?

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:41.080 line:91%
Well, the light arrives
in the delay lines

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:42.560 line:85%
<i>in that tunnel from the side.</i>

00:20:42.560 --> 00:20:44.480 line:85%
<i>It hits a series of mirrors</i>

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:46.120 line:91%
<i>designed to synchronise
all the beams,</i>

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:47.520 line:91%
<i>and each beam is
bounced up the tunnel</i>

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.320 line:91%
<i>to one of the carriages
and reflected back.</i>

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.000 line:91%
So how precise does
that have to be?

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:54.200 line:91%
<c.color88BBFF>I can show you.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>Okay.</c>

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:56.400 line:91%
<c.color88BBFF>We use the delay lines
for observation,</c>

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.840 line:91%
<c.color88BBFF>and you can see here
the error.</c>

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:02.840 line:85%
136 nanometres.

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.080 line:91%
I assumed that the carriage
would be accurate

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:07.160 line:85%
to maybe a millimetre?

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:09.800 line:91%
And then there'd be some
electronics and mirrors in there

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.000 line:91%
that would do the
nanometre correction.

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.560 line:91%
But is that carriage
accurate to

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:16.720 line:91%
100 nanometres
in its position?

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:18.600 line:91%
<c.color88BBFF>The error that
I showed you there</c>

00:21:18.600 --> 00:21:21.680 line:91%
<c.color88BBFF>is the precision that
the carriage has.</c>

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.320 line:91%
I understand why
I got told not to even

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.240 line:91%
get close to that line.
That's incredible!

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.520 line:91%
The carriages
can be moved with

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:31.160 line:91%
100 nanometres of precision.
100 nanometres!

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:32.640 line:91%
<i>That's why they didn't
want me near them.</i>

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:34.440 line:91%
<i>They'll have to recalibrate
each night anyway, of course,</i>

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:35.800 line:91%
<i>and it'll get cleaned
from time to time,</i>

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:38.400 line:85%
<i>but they don't want some idiot</i>

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:39.880 line:85%
<i>potentially bumping into things</i>

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:42.840 line:91%
<i>that have to be 100
nanometres accurate.</i>

00:21:42.840 --> 00:21:44.400 line:91%
<i>Because that's how
they delay the light</i>

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:45.520 line:85%
<i>and do optical interferometry.</i>

00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:47.080 line:91%
<i>That's how everything
is synchronised.</i>

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:48.280 line:85%
<i>No recording, no playback,</i>

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:50.400 line:85%
<i>no computers analysing things.</i>

00:21:50.400 --> 00:21:53.040 line:85%
<i>It's analogue. It's physical.</i>

00:21:53.040 --> 00:21:55.160 line:91%
<i>Explaining what happens in
the science part of that,</i>

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:57.200 line:91%
<i>inside the freezing
cold vacuum of...</i>

00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:59.040 line:91%
<i>science stuff,
in the rest of the lab,</i>

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:00.200 line:85%
<i>is way beyond me.</i>

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:02.240 line:91%
<i>That's the stuff astronomy
PhDs are made of.</i>

00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:03.440 line:85%
<i>And it's still a lot of work</i>

00:22:03.440 --> 00:22:04.960 line:91%
<i>to record the
interference patterns</i>

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.480 line:85%
<i>and work back from that.</i>

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:10.160 line:91%
<i>But, if all those beams
are synchronised enough...</i>

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.440 line:91%
it means they can
approximate a telescope

00:22:12.440 --> 00:22:15.120 line:91%
the size of the
entire VLT platform,

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.120 line:85%
<i>all four Unit Telescopes,</i>

00:22:17.120 --> 00:22:19.720 line:91%
<i>instead of just the
individual telescope mirrors.</i>

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:22.360 line:91%
<i>But all those
telescopes, for optical,</i>

00:22:22.360 --> 00:22:24.520 line:91%
have to be
physically connected.

00:22:24.520 --> 00:22:26.040 line:91%
So, we've covered
interferometry.

00:22:26.040 --> 00:22:27.480 line:91%
We've covered the
mirrors and resurfacing.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:28.720 line:91%
And we've covered
the sheer scale

00:22:28.720 --> 00:22:29.800 line:85%
of these telescopes.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.200 line:91%
You now know everything
you need to know

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:33.800 line:85%
to work out why the ELT

00:22:33.800 --> 00:22:35.600 line:91%
– the Extremely Large
Telescope –

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:38.720 line:91%
will probably be the
largest optical telescope

00:22:38.720 --> 00:22:40.720 line:91%
<i>ever constructed.
And that ELT...</i>

00:22:40.720 --> 00:22:42.400 line:85%
<i>is our final stop.</i>

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:46.280 line:91%
If you're wondering
why I'm rocking

00:22:46.280 --> 00:22:48.720 line:91%
the extremely fashionable
combination of

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:50.440 line:85%
awful raggedy old baseball cap

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.240 line:91%
that I found in my
checked baggage,

00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:54.440 line:85%
and hoodie up...

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:57.680 line:91%
The altitude is about
three kilometres here.

00:22:57.680 --> 00:22:59.840 line:85%
The UV index is just "yes"

00:22:59.840 --> 00:23:01.400 line:91%
'cause that's one of the
reasons they built here.

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:03.360 line:91%
We are as close to the
top of the atmosphere

00:23:03.360 --> 00:23:05.200 line:85%
as you can reasonably get.

00:23:05.600 --> 00:23:06.840 line:85%
<c.cyan>Let's go, guys.</c>

00:23:07.440 --> 00:23:10.360 line:91%
Oh, we've actually got named
safety gear in our size.

00:23:10.360 --> 00:23:11.640 line:85%
Okay, there we go.

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:13.760 line:91%
Required protective
equipment up here:

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:18.040 line:85%
Helmet, gloves, high vis,

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:22.160 line:91%
steel toe cap boots,
and factor 50 sunblock.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:24.080 line:91%
<i>The construction site
is 20 kilometres away.</i>

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.240 line:91%
<i>Down the hill that the VLT
is on, up the next one.</i>

00:23:26.240 --> 00:23:29.240 line:91%
<i>It is colossal, and it looks
so much closer than it is.</i>

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.160 line:91%
<i>My brain refused to admit
that a telescope dome</i>

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:32.480 line:85%
<i>could be that big.</i>

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:34.600 line:91%
<i>The construction
is 74 metres high,</i>

00:23:34.600 --> 00:23:36.240 line:85%
<i>86 metres in diameter.</i>

00:23:36.240 --> 00:23:37.400 line:85%
And that...

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.800 line:91%
That is an extremely
large telescope.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:43.600 line:85%
<c.green>The main mirror, the M1,</c>

00:23:43.600 --> 00:23:46.760 line:85%
<c.green>39.2 metres in diameter,</c>

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:51.160 line:91%
<c.green>comprised of
798 hexagonal panels.</c>

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:55.920 line:91%
<c.green>We will make the biggest
optical mirror ever built.</c>

00:23:56.240 --> 00:23:58.440 line:85%
That is... colossal!

00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:00.760 line:85%
<c.green>We are expecting images</c>

00:24:00.760 --> 00:24:03.240 line:91%
<c.green>five times sharper than
the James Webb Telescope</c>

00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:04.760 line:85%
<c.green>that is in outer space.</c>

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:07.280 line:91%
Sorry, five times sharper
than the James Webb?

00:24:07.280 --> 00:24:09.280 line:85%
The one that's out there

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:10.680 line:91%
<c.green>Yeah.</c>
<c.colorEEEEEE>beyond the atmosphere?</c>

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:11.760 line:85%
<c.green>Yeah, exactly.</c>

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:13.400 line:91%
<i>The first reason
that there's likely</i>

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:16.320 line:91%
<i>to be nothing bigger
than this is sheer cost.</i>

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:20.840 line:91%
<c.green>The cost line does not
follow a linear relationship,</c>

00:24:20.840 --> 00:24:22.280 line:85%
<c.green>but it goes exponential.</c>

00:24:22.280 --> 00:24:24.480 line:91%
<c.green>So maybe you will add,
you say,</c>

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:28.000 line:91%
<c.green>a couple of metres more,
and the costs double.</c>

00:24:28.000 --> 00:24:29.240 line:85%
<i>There have been plans</i>

00:24:29.240 --> 00:24:30.400 line:85%
for bigger telescopes.

00:24:30.400 --> 00:24:31.480 line:91%
There was something
called the

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:34.760 line:91%
Overwhelmingly Large
Telescope, the OWLT,

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:36.320 line:85%
that was briefly planned.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:37.400 line:85%
It could happen.

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:39.280 line:91%
But it'd cost
tens of billions of euro,

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:41.560 line:91%
and frankly, the
technology's unproven.

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:43.200 line:91%
Second reason:
We're reaching

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.200 line:91%
<i>the limits of what is
physically possible</i>

00:24:45.200 --> 00:24:46.800 line:91%
<i>with current
construction technology.</i>

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:48.120 line:91%
<i>They're engineering
something</i>

00:24:48.120 --> 00:24:51.360 line:91%
<i>that is part telescope,
part skyscraper.</i>

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:54.160 line:91%
So it's lots of different
sections all working together?

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:56.560 line:91%
<c.green>Yeah, all the sections
are working together,</c>

00:24:56.560 --> 00:25:00.240 line:91%
<c.green>and its geometry
will be readjusted</c>

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:02.680 line:91%
<c.green>through three actuators
per segment,</c>

00:25:02.680 --> 00:25:06.840 line:91%
<c.green>for a total number
of 2,394 actuators.</c>

00:25:06.840 --> 00:25:08.920 line:91%
<i>The eight metre
mirrors in the VLT,</i>

00:25:08.920 --> 00:25:10.360 line:91%
<i>they sag under
their own weight.</i>

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:12.520 line:91%
<i>And granted, the ELT is
using mirror segments,</i>

00:25:12.520 --> 00:25:14.600 line:91%
<i>not a single monolithic mirror,
but keeping them</i>

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:16.680 line:91%
all aligned isn't easy,
and the entire structure

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:19.320 line:91%
<i>will still stretch and warp
as it's moved around.</i>

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:21.640 line:91%
<i>And with thermal expansion
as the temperature changes.</i>

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:25.520 line:91%
<c.green>This wall is basically
the upper foundation,</c>

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.720 line:91%
<c.green>where on top of it,
the telescope will be mounted.</c>

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:29.800 line:85%
Right.

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:31.520 line:91%
<c.green>So this is
fixed to the ground,</c>

00:25:31.520 --> 00:25:35.160 line:91%
<c.green>and on top of it, there is
a hydraulic bearing system</c>

00:25:35.160 --> 00:25:37.680 line:85%
<c.green>that will be moving the telescope.</c>

00:25:37.680 --> 00:25:39.360 line:91%
How precise does
that have to be,

00:25:39.360 --> 00:25:40.640 line:85%
for those movements?

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:44.400 line:91%
<c.green>We are talking
about 100/1,000,</c>

00:25:44.400 --> 00:25:47.960 line:91%
<c.green>depending on the observation,
of arc seconds.</c>

00:25:47.960 --> 00:25:49.800 line:91%
<i>Okay, so what does
a thousandth of an</i>

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.720 line:91%
<i>arc second
of precision mean?</i>

00:25:51.720 --> 00:25:53.400 line:91%
<i>I did some maths,
and it means that</i>

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:55.520 line:91%
<i>this colossal
skyscraper telescope</i>

00:25:55.520 --> 00:25:58.120 line:91%
<i>will move so smoothly
and so precisely</i>

00:25:58.120 --> 00:26:01.000 line:91%
that if you told it to look at
and track a spot on the moon,

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.040 line:91%
it would be accurate
to within two metres!

00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:06.160 line:91%
The actual imaging resolution's
not quite that small.

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:07.960 line:91%
It could image buildings
about 10 metres across

00:26:07.960 --> 00:26:09.200 line:91%
at that distance,
but again...

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:12.520 line:91%
That's a distance that can be
measured in light seconds!

00:26:12.520 --> 00:26:14.800 line:91%
<i>We are at the limits of
what it's possible to do</i>

00:26:14.800 --> 00:26:16.240 line:85%
<i>with current technology.</i>

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:17.680 line:85%
<i>And the last reason</i>

00:26:17.680 --> 00:26:19.280 line:91%
<i>– and I think the
most important one –</i>

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:21.080 line:85%
<i>is that at some point,</i>

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:23.840 line:91%
<i>we're going to solve
optical interferometry.</i>

00:26:23.840 --> 00:26:25.160 line:85%
<i>People are working on it.</i>

00:26:25.160 --> 00:26:26.560 line:91%
<i>Right now, it still
has to be physical.</i>

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.360 line:91%
<i>Computers aren't fast enough.
Clocks aren't precise enough.</i>

00:26:29.640 --> 00:26:31.480 line:91%
<i>But they're only
going to get better.</i>

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:33.360 line:85%
<i>At some point in the future,</i>

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:35.560 line:91%
<i>we are probably going
to figure that out,</i>

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:37.880 line:91%
and we'll be able to do
optical interferometry

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:39.680 line:91%
the same way that
we can do radio.

00:26:39.680 --> 00:26:40.960 line:85%
<i>And as soon as that happens,</i>

00:26:40.960 --> 00:26:43.480 line:91%
<i>the Extremely Large
Telescope will be...</i>

00:26:44.240 --> 00:26:45.320 line:85%
not obsolete.

00:26:45.320 --> 00:26:47.560 line:91%
It'll still be useful for
some types of observations,

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:50.280 line:91%
because there are
advantages to the size.

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:53.320 line:91%
<i>Big telescopes let you
detect very small things</i>

00:26:53.320 --> 00:26:54.600 line:85%
<i>and very faint things.</i>

00:26:54.600 --> 00:26:56.480 line:91%
<i>Interferometry gets you
the small stuff,</i>

00:26:56.480 --> 00:26:57.880 line:85%
<i>but not the faint stuff,</i>

00:26:57.880 --> 00:26:59.360 line:91%
<i>because there's
less mirror surface</i>

00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:01.000 line:85%
<i>for the light to bounce off.</i>

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:02.480 line:85%
But odds are, at some point,

00:27:02.480 --> 00:27:05.200 line:91%
there'll be much cheaper
and easier virtual ways

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:07.320 line:91%
of making
really big telescopes.

00:27:07.320 --> 00:27:09.320 line:91%
The ELT is still going to do
a hell of a lot of science

00:27:09.320 --> 00:27:11.480 line:91%
before that can happen,
if it ever does happen.

00:27:11.480 --> 00:27:12.840 line:85%
<i>But once that's done,</i>

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.280 line:91%
<i>maybe in a few decades,
maybe a century,</i>

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.480 line:91%
<i>the case for building
something this big</i>

00:27:17.480 --> 00:27:19.280 line:91%
kind of goes away,
if instead,

00:27:19.280 --> 00:27:21.000 line:91%
you can build a few
small telescopes

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.680 line:85%
and piece them all together.

00:27:22.680 --> 00:27:25.760 line:91%
<i>So this, the Extremely
Large Telescope,</i>

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:29.640 line:91%
<i>will probably be the last of
the great optical telescopes.</i>

00:27:29.640 --> 00:27:31.040 line:91%
I could be wrong.
Who knows?

00:27:31.040 --> 00:27:33.000 line:91%
Maybe in a century's time,
this will seem ridiculous,

00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:34.800 line:91%
and there'll be a
100 metre mirror

00:27:34.800 --> 00:27:38.040 line:91%
sitting high on a plateau
somewhere in South America.

00:27:38.280 --> 00:27:40.000 line:85%
But if I had to place a bet,

00:27:40.000 --> 00:27:41.680 line:91%
I think there's a
very strong chance

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:45.280 line:91%
the ELT will be the
largest optical telescope

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:47.160 line:85%
to ever be built.

